{
  "decision": "edit",
  "issues": [
    {
      "quote": "The head is already bearing on the spring.",
      "problem": "The initial view shows the head very near the top coil, but direct bearing or contact is not clearly visible, so the contact claim is unsupported."
    },
    {
      "quote": "Throughout the excerpt the cylindrical head descends steadily",
      "problem": "The phrase uses the annotation word 'excerpt', and the visible descent is gradual rather than demonstrably steady."
    },
    {
      "quote": "the upper coils lean and buckle slightly to the right while the lower coils bulge outward",
      "problem": "'Buckle' and 'bulge' overstate the visible deformation; the turns mainly lean slightly and spread outward."
    },
    {
      "quote": "The base and wall remain fixed, confirming the camera is static and the motion belongs to the descending head.",
      "problem": "The base and wall staying in place does not confirm camera stillness, and the visible change also includes spring compression."
    },
    {
      "quote": "By the final frame",
      "problem": "This uses the annotation phrase 'final frame'."
    },
    {
      "quote": "Yet the descent continues and the process is still underway, with the spring not visibly settled into a finished state.",
      "problem": "Saying the descent continues beyond the last visible moment is unsupported; the visible action simply does not reach a settled state within the shown range."
    }
  ],
  "caption": "The video captures a close-up view of a black helical coil spring standing vertically between a cylindrical metal press head above and a hexagonal metal base platform below, set against a mottled gray concrete wall. A small yellow label marks the right edge of the base. The head is already close to the top coil.\n\nAs the action proceeds, the cylindrical head descends gradually, its lower edge moving from just above the top coil toward the compressed stack. The spring shortens as the gaps between its turns shrink until the coils close together. As compression advances, the upper coils lean slightly to the right while the lower coils spread outward. The base and wall stay in place, so the visible change is the head's descent and the spring's compression.\n\nBy the last visible moment the coil stack is heavily compressed and the head is close to it, while the turns remain partly separated and the process is still not visibly settled into a finished state within view.",
  "length_note": ""
}
